# Rule: Verify Stigmer Apps Changes

When triggered, analyze the current uncommitted or recently committed changes, map them to the relevant checks, and run only those. This avoids running the full CI gate when only a subset of the monorepo was touched.

## Execution

### Step 1: Identify changed files

Run `git diff --name-only HEAD~1` (if the last commit is the session's work) or `git diff --name-only` (if uncommitted). Use whichever captures the session's changes. Combine both if needed.

### Step 2: Map changes to checks

Run every check whose row matches **any** changed file. All commands run from the repo root.

| File pattern | Command | What it checks |
|---|---|---|
| Any change at all | `CUSTOMER_DENYLIST=... node scripts/check-customer-data.mjs` | No customer data anywhere — this repo is public (see note below) |
| `**/*.proto`, `buf.yaml`, `*/buf.gen.yaml` | `npm run codegen && git diff --exit-code -- packages/resource-api/src/gen apps/law/backend/src/gen` | Committed generated code matches the protos (CI fails on drift) |
| `packages/resource-api/src/**` | `npm run build -w @stigmer/resource-api && npm run typecheck && npm test -w @stigmer/resource-api` | Commons compiles, types hold repo-wide, commons tests pass |
| `apps/law/backend/**` | `npm run build && npm run typecheck -w @law/backend && npm test -w @law/backend` | App compiles against the built commons and its acceptance tests pass |
| `package.json` (any), `package-lock.json`, `tsconfig*` | `npm ci && npm run build && npm run typecheck` | Workspace wiring still resolves and compiles |
| `.github/workflows/**` | Visual review only | No local runner for workflow changes |

Ordering matters: **build the commons before typechecking or testing the app** — `@law/backend` resolves `@stigmer/resource-api` through the workspace link, whose exports point at `dist/`. The root `npm run build` already does this in the right order.

When in doubt about whether a check is needed, include it — a few extra minutes of checking is better than a broken CI pipeline. The full gate is: guard → `npm run codegen` + drift check → `npm run build` → `npm run typecheck` → `npm test`.

### Step 3: Run and report

Report results clearly:

```
Verify Results:
  ✅ customer-data guard          — passed
  ✅ codegen drift                — zero drift
  ✅ npm run build                — commons + app compiled
  ✅ npm test -w @law/backend     — 87 tests passed
```

If a check fails, show the error output so the user can fix it.

### Step 4: Handle pre-existing issues

If failures appear in files **not** touched by the current session, note them as pre-existing and do not treat them as blockers. Known instance: the notification newest-first ordering assertion in `apps/law/backend/src/__tests__/task-notification.integration.test.ts` can tie on same-millisecond timestamps (project DD-003).

## Notes

- **The guard needs `CUSTOMER_DENYLIST`** (newline- or comma-separated customer terms; ask the owner — the list is a secret precisely because committing it would spell out the names it blocks). Without the env var the script hard-fails by design. If the terms are unavailable locally, note that the denylist layer runs in CI and verify the generic layer's patterns don't fire.
- Integration tests need a running Docker daemon (Testcontainers: Postgres for both workspaces, MinIO for the document flow).
- `npm run codegen` uses buf remote plugins — it needs network access.
- There is no Makefile and no Bazel in this repo; npm scripts at the root are the single entry point for every check.
